        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-306",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Passkey assertions are signed with UP and UV set and no user interaction, so any script in an RP origin can silently get a verified assertion.\n\nThe source is a page-posted get request: the relay accepts any same-window message (relay.ts:72), and so does any script calling navigator.credentials.get. The sink is the signed assertion, whose UP flag is hard-coded and whose UV flag comes from INITIAL_PASSKEY_POLICY (presence 'always', verification 'always'). Production passes no `policy`, so the user-activation gate (request.ts:196) never applies. The Worker then signs the page-chosen challenge with nothing between the request and the private key: no presence check, no verification, no prompt.\n\nImpact: Turns any script execution on a relying party's origin into a silent, phishing-resistant MFA login (UV=1) to the victim's account from the attacker's device. Passkeys' user-presence and user-verification guarantees are lost, and the RP is told verification happened when it did not.\n\nExploit scenario: From their own machine the attacker starts a passkey login at https://victim.example and gets the server challenge. Script they injected into the victim's https://victim.example page calls navigator.credentials.get({publicKey:{challenge, rpId:'victim.example', allowCredentials:[{type:'public-key', id:<victim credential id>}]}}). The MAIN-world wrapper relays the request, the background admits it without user activation, selectPasskey finds the one listed credential, and the Worker signs authenticatorData with UP|UV|BE|BS set. The ADR 0021 D re-prompt does not apply because allowCredentials is not empty. No window or prompt appears. The relay posts the assertion to the page, the script sends it to the attacker, and the attacker completes the login on their device as the victim. A native WebAuthn request would need the user to interact with the browser's prompt.\n\nPreconditions: The attacker can run script in a top-level document of the RP origin, or of any subdomain that may claim the RP ID (XSS, a compromised third-party script, or a user-content subdomain not on the PSL); The victim's vault is unlocked (automatic unlock per ADR 0006) and holds one matching passkey for the RP ID, or a site default; a request with a non-empty allowCredentials also signs silently in a tab where Pateat signed before.\n\nRecommendation: This behaviour is recorded as an owner-chosen deviation in docs/adr/0007-existing-passkey-assertions.md (Presence and verification; Consequences). Triage it as an accepted risk, not a new defect. To reduce it: default to presence 'activation' and verification 'never', or require a Pateat-owned confirmation in the extension chooser window before each claimed assertion; never set UV without verifying the user; make unattended signing a per-site opt-in instead of the global default.\n\nCWE-306. Severity MEDIUM, confidence medium."
          },
