        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-306",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Any script on an HTTPS page can make Pateat silently sign a passkey assertion with UP and UV set, with no gesture or user verification.\n\nThe page sends the get request through the wrapper at page.ts:394 or by posting straight to the relay at relay.ts:121. The vault private key then signs an assertion that relay.ts:66 posts back to the page. The production runtime has no policy option (background.ts:309-326), so INITIAL_PASSKEY_POLICY applies: presence \"always\" and verification \"always\". As a result the only presence gate (request.ts:196) never runs, the relay's userActivation boolean is ignored, and assertion.ts:42-43 sets UP=1 and UV=1 even though the user did nothing.\n\nImpact: Script running in any top-level HTTPS page whose origin may use the RP ID can quietly get a valid, user-verified assertion over a challenge it chooses, for a vault passkey of that RP. Examples are XSS or a compromised third-party script. This means account takeover at the RP from an attacker-controlled session, and it bypasses step-up flows that set userVerification \"required\". Native WebAuthn would need the user to approve a browser prompt. Silent per-credential allowCredentials probes also show which of the site's credential IDs the user's vault holds.\n\nExploit scenario: An attacker has script execution on https://example.com through stored XSS or a malicious analytics or ad script. The script fetches a login challenge that the attacker's server got from example.com. It then calls navigator.credentials.get({publicKey:{challenge, allowCredentials:[]}}), or posts {channel:\"pateat.passkey.v1\", type:\"get\", id, request} straight to the isolated relay. Suppose the unlocked vault (kept unlocked across restarts per ADR 0006) holds one discoverable passkey for example.com, or a site default. The background then signs at once (runtime.ts:514) and no window or prompt opens. The relay posts {kind:\"assertion\"} to the window, the script sends it to the attacker, and the attacker's server finishes the WebAuthn login as the victim. A step-up request with userVerification:\"required\" is also admitted, because policy.verification is \"always\".\n\nPreconditions: Attacker-controlled JavaScript runs in a top-level HTTPS document whose origin can use the target RP ID (XSS, a third-party script, or control of a covered subdomain whose origin the RP accepts); The user's Pateat vault is unlocked and holds exactly one matching zero-counter passkey for the RP ID, or a site default for that origin; The site is not excluded in Pateat settings.\n\nRecommendation: Do not sign without proof of user presence. Make the production policy presence \"activation\" at minimum, so the relay's isolated-world userActivation gate applies. Set UV only after a real local verification step, or always clear it and delegate userVerification \"required\" requests. Better still, ask the user to confirm on first use per origin, or through Pateat's own window, before signing. This behavior is a documented owner decision (ADR 0007, 2026-10-10: knowingly departing from the WebAuthn ceremony, with a per-site setting planned for phase 3). Triage it as an accepted risk that should be revisited, not as an accidental bug.\n\nCWE-306. Severity MEDIUM, confidence medium."
          },
