        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-346",
          "ruleIndex": 1,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Opaque-origin (CSP/iframe sandboxed) documents are treated as their URL's origin, so vault secrets reach sandboxed user content.\n\nidentify() takes the document origin from the URL in sender.url (runtime.ts:2033/2040) and never compares it to the browser-reported sender.origin. In the content script, the only origin check (content.ts:800) compares against location.origin, which is the URL's origin even when the document's real origin is opaque. The passkey bridge does reject this case (passkeys/runtime.ts:201 requires URL.parse(sender.url)?.origin === sender.origin), but the login path has no such check. A page the site deliberately isolates with a CSP 'sandbox' header is therefore treated as the site itself for URI matching, generation and filling.\n\nImpact: Credential theft that crosses an isolation boundary the site set up on purpose. Sandboxed, attacker-controlled content gets the site's vault password and other bound fields.\n\nExploit scenario: victim.com serves user-uploaded HTML from its own host (e.g. /uploads/x.html) with 'Content-Security-Policy: sandbox allow-scripts' so that uploads run in an opaque origin, not as victim.com. The attacker uploads a page that renders a visible password form and listens for input events. When the victim opens it, Pateat's content script announces the document as https://victim.com. The worker finds the victim's victim.com vault item by URL, offers the top-level page for automatic recipe generation, and runs the generated recipe. The content-side origin check passes because location.origin is https://victim.com. The vault password is typed character by character into the attacker's input, and the attacker's sandboxed script reads it and sends it to its own server.\n\nPreconditions: The site serves attacker-controlled HTML on its own host under CSP sandbox (allow-scripts, or allow-forms for a form-post variant); The victim has a vault item matching the site and recipe generation is connected; The victim opens the uploaded page.\n\nRecommendation: In identify(), require sender.origin to be a string equal to new URL(sender.url).origin, as senderOrigin() in passkeys/runtime.ts already does, and return undefined otherwise. In the content script, also compare self.origin (the effective origin), not location.origin, with operation.document.origin before filling.\n\nCWE-346. Severity MEDIUM, confidence medium."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "apps/extension/src/login/runtime.ts",
                  "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
                },
                "region": {
