        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-1333",
          "ruleIndex": 3,
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "Quadratic-time regex /=+$/ on a decrypted passkey string of up to about 786K characters stalls the crypto Worker.\n\nThe untrusted source is a vault-stored passkey credentialId, userHandle or keyValue. Someone who can write a cipher into the victim's vault controls it. The only check before the sink is a 1,400,000-character cap. At the sink, the unanchored /=+$/u replace backtracks quadratically in V8 on a long run of '=' followed by any other character.\n\nImpact: Availability only. The crypto Worker for that connection makes no progress for up to 30 s and is then terminated, locking the unlocked vault session. Passkey, fill and catalog operations for the connection fail until it is reopened, and the attacker's item makes this repeatable. Secrets are neither disclosed nor modified.\n\nExploit scenario: An organization member with write access to a collection shared with the victim, on a server that accepts large encrypted passkey fields (for example a self-hosted Vaultwarden), creates a login item. Its passkey credentialId is \"b64.\" followed by roughly 700K '=' characters and then 'A', and its rpId is a site the victim uses. When the victim opens site accounts on that origin (site-accounts.ts -> findVaultPasskeys -> passkey-find) or a passkey sign is attempted, the Worker's findStoredPasskeys calls mapLocalPasskeyCredentials -> decodeBitwardenCredentialId -> decodeBitwardenBase64. About n^2/2 (around 2.5e11) backtracking steps block the single-threaded Worker. The offscreen supervisor then kills it at 30 s, which ends the unlocked session. This repeats every time the trigger fires.\n\nPreconditions: Attacker can place a cipher in the victim's vault: org membership with collection write access, or a hostile server able to supply an org key; The server accepts a passkey field whose encrypted form is hundreds of KB (the official Bitwarden server likely caps encrypted string lengths; not verified); The item's rpId matches an RP ID queried for an origin the victim uses (site-accounts lookup or passkey assertion); V8's backtracking regex engine is in use without the experimental linear-time fallback (default in Chrome; not verified by execution).\n\nRecommendation: Strip padding without a backtracking regex, for example by scanning from the end for at most two '=' characters, or reject the value up front unless it matches an anchored, linear pattern such as /^[A-Za-z0-9+/_-]*={0,2}$/u. Also bound credentialId and userHandle to their WebAuthn sizes (about 1366 and 88 base64 characters) in viewSchema before any decoding.\n\nCWE-1333. Severity LOW, confidence medium."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
